## 4. MUSCLE ACTIVATION HIGHLIGHTING

Identify the muscles genuinely involved in the exercise and highlight them using universal visual color coding:

- RED / ORANGE: Primary target muscles (prime movers).
- YELLOW: Secondary muscles (synergists / assistance).
- BLUE: Supporting and stabilizing muscles (fixators / core).
- NATURAL SKIN TONE: All other non-targeted muscles.

Overlay & Shader Requirements:

- Internal luminescence style: Highlights must appear as a subtle, translucent internal chromatic luminescence / glow beneath the skin surface, following actual anatomical muscle boundaries and muscle bellies, while keeping the real skin texture visible.
- Zero color spill: Highlights must stay strictly confined to targeted muscle tissue, with no color bleeding, halos, or chromatic aberration onto neutral skin, joints, shorts, floor, equipment, or the white background.
- Follow actual anatomical muscle boundaries and insertion points.
- Keep highlights locked to the correct muscles throughout contraction, elongation, and movement.
- Make highlights clearly visible without obscuring underlying muscle definition or skin texture.
- Reflect muscle engagement dynamically throughout the exercise repetition.
- Show bilateral (both sides) activation when appropriate.
- Do not highlight unrelated muscles.

Side-Specific & Active-Only Highlighting:

- The color highlight must clearly show that the muscle is being worked right now. Muscles light up only while they are actively performing the movement and return to natural skin tone when they are not working.
- Highlight only the body part, and the side of the body, that actually performs the work. For unilateral or alternating exercises (e.g. a single-arm dumbbell curl with the right arm, a one-arm row, a single-leg movement, alternating lunges), highlight only the working side. The idle or resting side stays in natural skin tone for the entire time it is not working.
- When the working side alternates (left, then right), the highlight must switch sides in sync with the movement. Only the side that is performing at that moment is lit.
- For bilateral exercises where both sides work together (e.g. barbell bench press, squat, deadlift), highlight both sides at the same time.
- Highlight intensity follows the effort of the movement: brightest during the active contraction (the lifting or working phase), softer during the lowering or eccentric phase, and faded or off during rest or when the muscle is not engaged.
- Never highlight a muscle or side that is merely stationary, relaxed, or not contributing to the current movement.
- Do not display muscle names, written anatomical labels, or language-specific text.

The video must communicate muscle activation visually and intuitively for users worldwide.

